in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I help some sports fans fix their  caching performance while we hit a  performance buzzer beater stay tuned to  find out  geeky sports is a website devoted to the  latest and greatest regarding your  favorite sci-fi fantasy based sports yes  they even have coverage of that  wizard-based broomstick soccer game with  the flying locust thing that people try  to catch anyhow their engineering team  reached out after they deployed a new  CDN and thumbnailing service only to see  a massive Cashville cost show up on  their cloud bill yet no performance  improvements showing up for their  customers now this all sounds like the  right setup so let's try to figure out  what's going on firstly to check that  the assets were actually being cashed we  took a look at the logging information  for the load balancer in the cloud  console each request that slog will have  information detailing if it properly had  a cache hit or not for example we can  see that the first request resulted in a  lookup a failure and a fill meanwhile  our second request to the same resource  results in just a cache hit looking back  at the logs for geeky sports we can see  that the number of cache hit entries is  really low for their traffic during the  day so it seems the requests were  getting cached but not being reused by  future requests hence the high cache  fill bill now let's think about this for  a second at their core CDNs are really  just massively distributed hash tables  when a request comes in to the CDN it  converts the URI of that request to a  cache key if that key matches anything  that's already been cached then it just  returns the Associated object now by  default Google Cloud CDN uses the  complete request URI as the cache key  which means if you change the protocol  like moving from HTTP to HTTPS you're  not going to get a cache hit and  likewise if the query string is  different you won't get a match either  and that last one seems to be the  important one looking back at the logged  requests geeky sports is passing the  user token as a URL parameter was  causing each asset to be cached by the  CDN individually rather than being  shared between users thankfully for our  geeky friends the fix is pretty  straightforward using Google Cloud CDNs  custom cache keys we can modify how the  CDN matches you  our eyes for specific assets and tell it  to ignore the query string looking at  the logs we can see that even though the  user ID is different we got a proper hit  on the asset to learn more about how  geeky sports decrease to their costs  check out the Related article linked in  the description below and don't forget  to check out the rest of our cloud  performance Atlas content because when  it comes to performance every  millisecond counts
 

